      "worked": "Let $y=2^{\\sin^2x}>0$. Since $\\cos^2x=1-\\sin^2x$, $2^{\\cos^2x}=\\frac2y$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y+\\frac2y&=3\\\\y^2-3y+2&=0\\\\(y-1)(y-2)&=0\\\\y&=1\\text{ or }2\\\\2^{\\sin^2x}&=1\\text{ or }2\\\\\\sin^2x&=0\\text{ or }1\\\\\\sin x&=0,1\\text{ or }-1\\\\x&=0,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt each listed angle, $\\sin^2x$ and $\\cos^2x$ are 0 and 1 in some order, giving $1+2=3$. Both endpoints are included.",
